Here is a section-by-section summary of Title IX (Committee on Homeland Security and Governmental Affairs) of the bill:

**Subtitle A—Homeland Security Provisions**

**Sec. 90001. Border infrastructure and wall system.**
Appropriates $46,550,000,000 to the Commissioner of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) for fiscal year 2025, available through September 30, 2029. The funds cover construction, installation, or improvement of new or replacement primary, waterborne, and secondary barriers; access roads; barrier system attributes such as cameras, lights, sensors, and other detection technology; and ground preparation work near the border to enable CBP operations.

**Sec. 90002. U.S. Customs and Border Protection personnel, fleet vehicles, and facilities.**
Appropriates $4,100,000,000 to CBP (through FY 2029) to hire and train additional Border Patrol agents, Office of Field Operations officers, Air and Marine agents, rehired annuitants, and field support personnel, plus $2,052,630,000 for recruitment, performance, and retention bonuses for those agent categories. It also provides $855,000,000 for repairing, leasing, or acquiring patrol vehicles and $5,000,000,000 for leasing, acquiring, constructing, or improving CBP facilities and checkpoints. None of these funds may be used after October 31, 2028, to recruit, hire, or train personnel for processing coordinator duties.

**Sec. 90003. Detention capacity.**
Appropriates $45,000,000,000 to U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE) for FY 2025, available through September 30, 2029, for single adult alien detention capacity and family residential center capacity. Aliens may be detained at family residential centers pending removal decisions under the Immigration and Nationality Act and until removal is completed, while detention standards for single adults are set at the discretion of the Secretary of Homeland Security. A "family residential center" is defined as a DHS facility detaining family units, including alien children who are not unaccompanied alien children.

**Sec. 90004. Border security, technology, and screening.**
Appropriates $6,168,000,000 to the CBP Commissioner for FY 2025 (available through September 30, 2029) for eight purposes, including nonintrusive inspection equipment using AI and machine learning to combat narcotics at ports of entry and along the southwest, northern, and maritime borders; Air and Marine platform upgrades; border surveillance technology; the biometric entry/exit system; screening of persons entering or exiting; initial screenings of unaccompanied alien children under the 2008 Wilberforce Act; combating fentanyl and precursor trafficking; and commemorating border security events. Funds may not be used for surveillance towers on the southwest or northern borders that have not been tested and accepted by CBP as delivering autonomous capabilities. "Autonomous" is defined as a system using AI, machine learning, computer vision, or other algorithms to detect, identify, classify, and track items of interest in real time and make operational adjustments without continuous human control.

**Sec. 90005. State and local assistance.**
Appropriates to the FEMA Administrator for FY 2025 (through September 30, 2029), under the State Homeland Security Grant Program: $500,000,000 for detecting and monitoring unmanned aircraft system threats, $625,000,000 for 2026 FIFA World Cup security, $1,000,000,000 for 2028 Olympics security and planning, and $450,000,000 for the Operation Stonegarden Grant Program, with the World Cup and Olympics funds exempt from certain Homeland Security Act requirements. It also establishes a "State Border Security Reinforcement Fund" in DHS with a $10,000,000,000 appropriation (available through September 30, 2034) for grants to States and local governments for border wall/fencing/buoy construction, ground preparation, detection and interdiction of illicit substances and criminally convicted unlawfully present aliens, and relocation of unlawfully present aliens from small population centers. Grants may cover expenditures made on or after January 20, 2021; States must apply with stated purposes and allocation plans, with applications accepted within 90 days of enactment; FEMA may retain up to 1 percent for administration; and nothing authorizes States or localities to exercise federal immigration or border security authorities.

**Sec. 90006. Presidential residence protection.**
Appropriates $300,000,000 to the FEMA Administrator for FY 2025 (through September 30, 2029) to reimburse State and local law enforcement personnel costs for protection activities at any residence of the President designated under sections 3 or 4 of the Presidential Protection Assistance Act of 1976 to be secured by the U.S. Secret Service. Reimbursable costs must be incurred on or after July 1, 2024, exceed typical law enforcement operating costs, be directly attributable to that protection at a nongovernmental property, and be certified as Secret Service-requested activities. FEMA may use up to 3 percent of the funds for grant administration.

**Sec. 90007. Department of Homeland Security appropriations for border support.**
Appropriates $10,000,000,000 to the Secretary of Homeland Security for FY 2025, available through September 30, 2029, to reimburse costs incurred in activities supporting DHS's mission of safeguarding U.S. borders.

**Subtitle B—Governmental Affairs Provisions**

**Sec. 90101. FEHB improvements.**
Cited as the "FEHB Protection Act of 2025," this section directs the Office of Personnel Management (OPM) Director, within 1 year of enactment, to issue regulations verifying the veracity of qualifying life events and confirming that family members added to Federal Employees Health Benefits (FEHB) Program plans are eligible, including during open seasons. It requires fraud risk assessments to cover ineligible enrollees, a 3-year comprehensive audit (starting 1 year after enactment) of covered family members using marriage and birth certificates, and—within 180 days—a process to disenroll or remove ineligible individuals. It amends 5 U.S.C. 8909 to exempt the required set-aside from annual congressional limitations and provides $66,000,000 in FY 2026 (derived from contributions, available through FY 2035) to fund the verification, audit, and disenrollment requirements.

**Sec. 90102. Pandemic Response Accountability Committee.**
Appropriates $88,000,000 for FY 2026 (available until expended) to the Pandemic Response Accountability Committee for oversight of the Coronavirus response and of pandemic-related funds provided in this Act or any other Act. It amends Section 15010 of the CARES Act to add this reconciliation Act to the Committee's oversight scope and extends the Committee's termination date from 2025 to 2034.

**Sec. 90103. Appropriation for the Office of Management and Budget.**
Appropriates $100,000,000 to the Office of Management and Budget for FY 2025, available through September 30, 2029, for purposes of finding budget and accounting efficiencies in the executive branch. [end of text]


